  "ID": "9.3",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Compare the differential genes on global proteome level and glycoproteome level for IPMN vs ND.  Provide a pathway level summary of biological functions of upregulated or downregulated glycopeptides whose global protein expression remained unchanged. ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 15:53:34",
  "elapsed_seconds": 417.92,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThe study compares intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMN) to normal duct tissues (ND) using global proteome and glycoproteome analyses. The glycoproteome dataset highlights differentially regulated glycopeptides, while the global proteome dataset identifies total protein expression changes. The goal is to identify pathways enriched in glycopeptides with unchanged global protein expression, suggesting post-translational regulation.  \n\n### Keywords  \n- **Glycoproteins**  \n- **Lysosome**  \n- **Glycosylation**  \n- **IPMN**  \n- **Post-translational modification**  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n- **ATP6V0A2** (lysosomal proton pump)  \n- **GUSB** (β-glucuronidase, lysosomal enzyme)  \n- **GNPTAB** (N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphate transferase)  \n- **ST6GALNAC1** (glycosyltransferase)  \n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n**Over-Representation Analysis (ORA)** of 18 glycopeptides with unchanged global expression revealed significant enrichment in lysosomal and glycosylation-related pathways:  \n\n#### **KEGG_2021_Human (UP)**  \n| Theme                     | Direction | Leading Genes                          |  \n|--------------------------|-----------|----------------------------------------|  \n| Lysosome                 | Up        | GNPTAB, ATP6V0A2, GUSB                 |  \n| Glycosaminoglycan degradation | Up        | GUSB                                   |  \n| Mucin type O-glycan biosynthesis | Up        | ST6GALNAC1                             |  \n\n#### **MSigDB_Hallmark_2020 (UP)**  \n| Theme                     | Direction | Leading Genes                          |  \n|--------------------------|-----------|----------------------------------------|  \n| Cholesterol Homeostasis  | Up        | GUSB                                   |  \n\n#### **Key Findings**  \n1. **Lysosome (KEGG)**:  \n   - Enriched with **3 genes** (GNPTAB, ATP6V0A2, GUSB), all involved in lysosomal enzyme trafficking or degradation.  \n   - **GNPTAB** encodes a subunit of GlcNAc-phosphotransferase, critical for lysosomal enzyme targeting.  \n   - **ATP6V0A2** is a lysosomal proton pump subunit, linking pH regulation to glycoprotein processing.  \n\n2. **Glycosylation Pathways**:  \n   - **GUSB** (β-glucuronidase) is central to glycosaminoglycan (GAG) degradation and mucin-type O-glycan biosynthesis.  \n   - **ST6GALNAC1** catalyzes sialylation of O-glycans, dysregulated in cancer glycosylation.  \n\n3. **Cholesterol Homeostasis (Hallmark)**:  \n   - **GUSB** overlaps with this pathway, suggesting cross-talk between lysosomal function and lipid metabolism.  \n\n---\n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \n- **Lysosomal genes**: GNPTAB, ATP6V0A2, GUSB (FDR < 0.003–0.085).  \n- **Glycosylation enzymes**: ST6GALNAC1, GUSB (FDR < 0.03–0.085).  \n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n| Gene       | Direction | Role/Function                              | Pathway(s)                     |  \n|------------|-----------|--------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|  \n| **GNPTAB** | Up        | Lysosomal enzyme trafficking               | Lysosome (KEGG)                |  \n| **ATP6V0A2** | Up      | Lysosomal proton pump subunit              | Lysosome, Phagosome (KEGG)     |  \n| **GUSB**   | Up        | GAG degradation, O-glycan processing       | Lysosome, Cholesterol (Hallmark)|  \n| **ST6GALNAC1** | Up    | Sialylation of O-glycans                   | Mucin-type O-glycans (KEGG)    |  \n\n---\n\n### Biological Implications  \n1. **Lysosomal Dysregulation**:  \n   - Upregulated lysosomal proteins (GNPTAB, ATP6V0A2, GUSB) suggest hyperactive lysosomal function in IPMN, potentially supporting tumor cell survival via enhanced degradation of extracellular matrix or immune evasion.  \n\n2. **Glycosylation Alterations**:  \n   - **ST6GALNAC1** and **GUSB** indicate dysregulated O-glycosylation, a hallmark of mucin-producing tumors like IPMN. This may alter cell adhesion, signaling, or immune recognition.  \n\n3. **Metabolic Rewiring**:  \n   - Enrichment in cholesterol homeostasis (via **GUSB**) links lysosomal activity to lipid metabolism, which is critical for tumor cell membrane biogenesis and signaling.  \n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate lysosomal activity** in IPMN using functional assays (e.g., lysosomal pH or enzyme activity assays).  \n2. **Investigate O-glycan structures** on mucins (e.g., MUC5AC) to confirm ST6GALNAC1-driven sialylation.  \n3. **Explore therapeutic targeting** of lysosomal enzymes (e.g., GUSB inhibitors) in preclinical models.  \n\n---\n\n### References  \n1. PMID 31537899: Lysosomal dysfunction in cancer progression.  \n2. PMID 29686335: Role of ST6GALNAC1 in cancer glycosylation.  \n3. PMID 30085230: Cholesterol metabolism in pancreatic cancer.  \n\n--- \n\nThis analysis highlights lysosomal and glycosylation pathways as key drivers in IPMN, with potential implications for biomarker development and targeted therapy.",
